[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000843","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000843","source_record_id":"rec_000843","source_corpus":"include","title":"mRNA-LM: full-length integrated SLM for mRNA analysis","abstract":"The success of SARS-CoV-2 (severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2) messenger RNA (mRNA) vaccine has led to increased interest in the design and use of mRNA for vaccines and therapeutics. Still, selecting the most appropriate mRNA sequence for a protein remains a challenge. Se v eral recent studies ha v e sho wn that the specific mRNA sequence can ha v e a significant impact on the translation efficiency, halflife, degradation rates, and other issues that play a major role in determining vaccine efficiency. To enable the selection of the most appropriate sequence, w e de v eloped mRNA-LM, an integrated small language model f or modeling the entire mRNA sequence. mRNA-LM uses the contrastive language-image pretraining integration technology to combine three separate language models for the diff erent mRNA segments. W e trained mRNA-LM on millions of diverse mRNA sequences from se v eral different species. The unsupervised model was able to learn meaningful biology related to e v olution and host-pathogen interactions. Fine-tuning of mRNA-LM allo w ed us to use it in se v eral mRNA property prediction tasks. As we show, using the full-length integrated model led to accurate predictions, improving on prior methods proposed for this task.","doi":"10.1093/nar/gkaf044","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source: Materials and methods > Datasets for pretraining UTRBERTs]\nWe used several datasets to construct the input for our models. These included Mammalia reference sequences ( https://www. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ datasets/ taxonomy/ 40674/ ), human virus with complete nucleotides ( https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ labs/ virus/ vssi/ #/ ), Esc heric hia coli ( https://www.ncbi.nlm. nih.gov/ datasets/ taxonomy/ 562/ ), and yeast ( https:// www. yeastgenome.org/). Both the NCBI and yeast genome websites only provide the full mRNA sequences and the CDSs. To identify the UTR sequences, we aligned the CDS with the full sequence. The section preceding the coding region was designated as the 5 ′ UTR, while the section following it was labeled as the 3 ′ UTR. Following these steps, we ended up with 10 million coding, 6 million 5 ′ UTR, and 4 million 3 ′ UTR sequences. The number of unique sequences for each family can be found in Supplementary Table S1 .\n\n[input_representation: Materials and methods > Pretraining our models]\nSee CodonBERT [ 22 ] for detailed information on how we trained the CDS component. In contrast to the coding module, which uses codons as the tokens, both UTRBERTs are nucleotide-based models and treat the nucleotides as tokens directly without any special tokenization. The BERT model we used for these regions follows the model we used for CodonBERT [ 22 ] and has 12 hidden layers, 12 attention heads, a hidden size of 768, and an intermediate size of 3072. The main difference between the two architectures is the maximum sequence length. We set it to 512 for 5UTRBERT since &gt; 80% of the training sequences are shorter than 500 nt. In contrast, we set it to 1024 for 3UTRBERT since 3 ′ UTR sequences are much longer than 5 ′ UTR. The pretraining of two models was performed using masking language model (MLM) optimization [ 31 ]. MLM is a self-supervised learning task that randomly masks 15% of the input tokens with the [MASK] token and predicts the masked tokens based on the remaining context."}]

